 # Ray-Centric Adaptive Step and Termination
 
-Also called: Adaptive Step Size, Adaptive Ray Marching, Adaptive Volume Integration
+Also called: Adaptive Ray Marching, Adaptive Step Size Control, Early Ray Termination (ERT)
 
 ## Overview
-Ray-Centric Adaptive Step and Termination is a family of volume rendering optimization techniques that dynamically adjust the sampling density along individual rays based on local scene complexity or accumulated opacity. Unlike uniform sampling, which casts a constant number of samples per ray regardless of content, this method reduces computational cost by increasing step sizes in homogeneous or empty regions and decreasing them near high-frequency details or surfaces [S1, S2, S3, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Two-stage NeRF Rendering](925-two-stage-nerf-rendering.md) by operating directly on the ray integration loop rather than separating coarse and fine passes, and from [Resolution Adaptation](3063-resolution-adaptation.md) by modulating the 1D step size along the ray rather than the 2D image resolution.
+Ray-Centric Adaptive Step and Termination optimizes volume rendering by dynamically adjusting the sampling density along individual rays and terminating evaluation when opacity thresholds are met, rather than using fixed uniform steps. It solves the inefficiency of uniform sampling, which wastes queries in empty regions and oversamples homogeneous areas, while ensuring visual fidelity in high-frequency regions. Unlike sibling methods that focus on global resolution scaling or random pixel subsampling, this family adapts parameters locally per ray based on metrics such as opacity, gradient magnitude, or distance from the viewpoint.
 
